.TH CCOL_MEMPOOL_CREATE_FROM_PREALLOCATED_BUFFER 3 "2026-08-28" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_mempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer \- create pool from user buffer
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cmempool.h>

ccol_mempool *ccol_mempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer(
    void *buffer,
    size_t buf_size,
    size_t elem_size,
    bool fallback_to_dynamic_memory,
    bool single_threaded,
    ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *mmgmt_procs,
    char **err);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Creates a memory pool on top of a buffer from the caller, so the pool
does not allocate from the heap. This is useful on embedded systems, and
whenever you do not want a heap allocation.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
buffer                     Pointer to the preallocated buffer
buf_size                   Size of the buffer in bytes
elem_size                  Size of each element in bytes. It must be
                          > 0. If it is smaller than sizeof(uintptr_t),
                          the library rounds it up, in the same way as
                          ccol_mempool_create(). The maximum is
                          SIZE_MAX less the stride rounding
fallback_to_dynamic_memory If true, allocate from the heap when the pool
                          is empty
single_threaded           If true, do not lock
mmgmt_procs               Custom memory management, or NULL
err                       Optional pointer that receives an error string
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns a pointer to the new memory pool on success
Returns NULL on failure
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The element count is buf_size / (stride + 1); an entry carries no
  header. The stride is elem_size rounded up to 16 bytes, and then
  further up to a power of two unless the library was built with
  -DCCOL_MEMPOOL_COMPACT_LAYOUT=1, so every element in the pool, not
  only the first one, is aligned for any object type. The one extra
  byte for each element is the status byte of that element, which stays
  in the same buffer, after the entries.
  CCOL_DECLARE_PREALLOCATED_MEMPOOL_BUFFER sizes its own buffer with the
  same arithmetic, which makes it the way to get the size right without
  a second statement of the formula
ccol_mempool_destroy() does not free the buffer; the caller owns the
  lifetime of the buffer
The pool struct itself still comes from mmgmt_procs
The pool calls the functions of mmgmt_procs for its dynamic fallback
entries, and for its own bookkeeping of them, in the middle of an update
of its own state, with its lock held when it has one. Those functions
must therefore not call back into this same pool: with a lock the call
deadlocks, and without one it corrupts that state. An allocator built on
a pool must draw from a different pool
Returns NULL with an error if elem_size is zero
If elem_size is nonzero but < sizeof(uintptr_t), the library rounds it
  up, as ccol_mempool_create() does
Returns NULL with an error if elem_size would overflow size_t after the
stride rounding
Returns NULL with an error if the buffer is not aligned to at least 16
  bytes. Every entry that the pool hands out is aligned to 16 bytes, and
  entry 0 sits at the address of the buffer, so the buffer must meet
  that alignment. A buffer that comes from
  CCOL_DECLARE_PREALLOCATED_MEMPOOL_BUFFER already meets it
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR CCOL_DECLARE_PREALLOCATED_MEMPOOL_BUFFER (3),
.BR ccol_mempool_create (3),
.BR ccol_mempool_destroy (3)
